course: dictionary
lesson: 5202
title: Words from Jamaica
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Jamaican means from or belonging to Jamaica, an island country in the Caribbean. As an adjective it describes a thing, as in «Jamaican food». As a noun it names a person: a Jamaican is someone from Jamaica, and Jamaicans is the plural.
explain: Context tells you which one you have. In «Jamaican Patois, also called Jamaican Creole, grew from English mixed with West African languages», Jamaican describes the Patois, so it is an adjective. Swap in your meaning, from Jamaica, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: A language can carry a whole history. Jamaican Patois is spoken by millions and has its own grammar, so it is not just English said badly. «Wagwan» means what's going on, or hello, and it moved from Jamaican Creole into the speech of young Londoners.
explain: Words also show where people's families came from. «Most Jamaicans today descend from Africans enslaved on British plantations», so the history of Jamaica links to Africa and to Britain. A dictionary tells you what a word means; the sentences around it tell you the story.
fact: Jamaican can be an adjective, as in «Jamaican food», or a noun, as in «a Jamaican».
fact: «Jamaican Patois» is also called «Jamaican Creole», and it grew from English mixed with West African languages.
fact: «Mi soon come» means I'll be there soon, though maybe later.
fact: «Wagwan» means what's going on, or hello, and «fam» means a close friend.
fact: Jamaica is an island country in the Caribbean, known for reggae music, Bob Marley, and food like jerk chicken and ackee and saltfish.
exercise: «Jamaican food» is on the menu. What kind of word is Jamaican here? | *Adjective, because it describes the food | Noun, because it names a person | Adverb, because it tells how | Verb, because it shows an action
exercise: «Two Jamaicans joined our class today.» What is Jamaicans in this sentence? | An adjective, describing the class | *A noun, meaning people from Jamaica | A verb, meaning to join | A place, meaning the island itself
exercise: «Mi soon come» is Jamaican Patois. What does it mean? | Come and eat with me now | I am going to Jamaica tomorrow | I am already here | *I'll be there soon, though maybe later
test: «Jamaican Patois grew from English mixed with West African languages.» Which two languages were mixed to make it? | *English and West African languages | Chinese and Jamaican | Latin and Greek | French and Spanish
test: In Birmingham, Grandma says «Wah gwaan, mi baby?». What does this mean? | Where is my baby hiding? | Did you eat my baby? | *What's going on, my baby? | Goodbye, my baby
exam: Which sentence uses Jamaican as a noun? | I love Jamaican food | Jamaican Patois has its own grammar | *Many Jamaicans have family roots in the UK | We played Jamaican music in class
